WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.363 --> 00:00:06.877
Hi, welcome back to Arya Nagarjuna.

00:00:06.877 --> 00:00:09.840
If you remember in the last class,

00:00:09.840 --> 00:00:13.063
we had gone through all the these trios.

00:00:13.063 --> 00:00:17.813
We were trying to figure out
if you could have, for example,

00:00:17.813 --> 00:00:20.127
the present and the future time

00:00:20.127 --> 00:00:22.138
if you didn't have the past time.

00:00:22.138 --> 00:00:23.524
So do the present and the future

00:00:23.524 --> 00:00:26.988
only exist relative to the past?

00:00:26.988 --> 00:00:30.925
And is that how the present
and the future exist,

00:00:30.925 --> 00:00:34.675
through relation to the
past? Do the concepts

00:00:35.661 --> 00:00:38.828
of past, present and future only exist

00:00:39.773 --> 00:00:44.610
because of the comparison
between past, present and future?

00:00:44.610 --> 00:00:47.887
And basically Nagarjuna says, "No."

00:00:47.887 --> 00:00:51.643
Because, a comparison
between, for example,

00:00:51.643 --> 00:00:54.209
present and future with past

00:00:54.209 --> 00:00:57.433
would require that in some sense,

00:00:57.433 --> 00:00:59.676
present and future would
have to make make contact

00:00:59.676 --> 00:01:02.617
with past for there to
be a valid comparison.

00:01:02.617 --> 00:01:05.857
There would have to be
some point of relationship.

00:01:05.857 --> 00:01:08.464
And that, gets into touchy territory

00:01:08.464 --> 00:01:11.988
because then something
which is present or future

00:01:11.988 --> 00:01:16.671
would have to, would have to be touching
something which were past,
23
00:01:16,671 --&gt; 00:01:19,807
and that's not viable.
It just doesn't work out.

00:01:19.807 --> 00:01:21.773
Then the edge of the present
thing would have to be

00:01:21.773 --> 00:01:25.259
the past also, to have a
relationship with the past.

00:01:25.259 --> 00:01:27.743
There has to be a point of contact.

00:01:27.743 --> 00:01:31.904
And you know, I think it's pretty natural

00:01:31.904 --> 00:01:36.654
for us to believe that the
concept of present and future,

00:01:37.682 --> 00:01:41.582
for example, relies upon
the concept of the past.

00:01:41.582 --> 00:01:44.987
And how we establish
things as being future

00:01:44.987 --> 00:01:48.212
or present or past in our own life

00:01:48.212 --> 00:01:50.860
is done by comparison of the three.

00:01:50.860 --> 00:01:53.172
And Nagarjuna is trying to point out

00:01:53.172 --> 00:01:57.129
that that's untenable.
There cannot be a comparison

00:01:57.129 --> 00:01:59.409
between past, present and future.

00:01:59.409 --> 00:02:01.659
So the very concept of time

00:02:02.529 --> 00:02:04.585
must be coming from someplace else.

00:02:04.585 --> 00:02:08.605
Not by comparison, not
because of comparison.

00:02:08.605 --> 00:02:12.855
And he said you can apply
that idea to other triads

00:02:13.797 --> 00:02:15.641
or other groups of three.

00:02:15.641 --> 00:02:19.808
For example, good karma,
bad karma, neutral karma.

00:02:21.549 --> 00:02:24.897
He had some other examples, what's that?

00:02:24.897 --> 00:02:27.980
Oh, best, worst and medium qualities.

00:02:29.738 --> 00:02:30.940
What was the other one?

00:02:30.940 --> 00:02:31.989
[Student] Numbers.

00:02:31.989 --> 00:02:34.841
Oh yeah, even one two and
three depend on a comparison.

00:02:34.841 --> 00:02:39.325
And we tend to inherently
believe that, for example,

00:02:39.325 --> 00:02:43.437
that the difference between
one and two and three

00:02:43.437 --> 00:02:45.895
depends on the comparison of the three.

00:02:45.895 --> 00:02:47.473
And Nagarjuna is trying to say

00:02:47.473 --> 00:02:49.093
that's not even possible.

00:02:49.093 --> 00:02:53.833
So where does even the concept
of a progression of time,

00:02:53.833 --> 00:02:56.713
how can time go from one
o'clock to two o'clock,

00:02:56.713 --> 00:02:58.857
if it's not by comparison?

00:02:58.857 --> 00:03:00.865
Where is it coming from?

00:03:00.865 --> 00:03:03.476
And we'll get into it,
we'll get into the answer.

00:03:03.476 --> 00:03:06.559
But first, he promised us two things:

00:03:09.066 --> 00:03:12.233
he's going to examine concepts of time

00:03:13.083 --> 00:03:17.000
from non-Buddhists schools
of Nagarjuna's time.

00:03:18.424 --> 00:03:22.566
So it would be like us talking
about what modern physicists

00:03:22.566 --> 00:03:25.669
think about time. In Nagarjuna's time,

00:03:25.669 --> 00:03:28.474
if he wanted to have some juicy debate,

00:03:28.474 --> 00:03:31.594
he would talk about the
idea of time, for example,

00:03:31.594 --> 00:03:34.206
in the Sankhya Hindu school, or something.

00:03:34.206 --> 00:03:37.953
And then by discussing their idea of time,

00:03:37.953 --> 00:03:41.344
our idea of time would become more clear.

00:03:41.344 --> 00:03:45.545
So first he promises a discussion of time

00:03:45.545 --> 00:03:47.378
from other traditions.

00:03:48.740 --> 00:03:53.346
And then he says, "Then let's
look at the concept of time

00:03:53.346 --> 00:03:57.992
in other traditions of Buddhism,
other than Middle Way,"

00:03:57.992 --> 00:04:00.590
and he's going to take
DNGOS SMRA BA, which means,

00:04:00.590 --> 00:04:04.402
functionalist, which
means, people who think

00:04:04.402 --> 00:04:08.295
that if something does something,
it should have some kind

00:04:08.295 --> 00:04:11.370
of inherent existence.

00:04:11.370 --> 00:04:14.699
If fire can be hot, then that heat

00:04:14.699 --> 00:04:17.949
must somehow be inherent to fire.

00:04:19.275 --> 00:04:22.531
Fires are never cold,
for example, freezing.

00:04:22.531 --> 00:04:24.407
Hmm...so here we go.

00:04:24.407 --> 00:04:27.751
This next verse, which is verse number five,

00:04:27.751 --> 00:04:32.084
is going to present the
idea of another school,

00:04:34.155 --> 00:04:39.155
another tradition of India
in the 2nd century A.D.

00:04:39.359 --> 00:04:40.192
And here's the verse,

00:04:40.192 --> 00:04:42.266
,MI GNAS DUS NI 'DZIN MI BYED,

00:04:42.266 --> 00:04:43.974
,GANG ZHIG GZUNG BAR BYA BA'I DUS,

00:04:43.974 --> 00:04:47.060
,GNAS PA YOD PA MA YIN PAS,

00:04:47.060 --> 00:04:49.418
,MA BZUNG DUS NI JI LTAR GDAGS,

00:04:50.355 --> 00:04:55.355
When you see GDAGS, G-D-A-G-S,
or any variation of it,

00:04:56.517 --> 00:04:58.995
you automatically have to be suspicious.

00:04:58.995 --> 00:05:01.154
What does it mean?

00:05:01.154 --> 00:05:06.154
It means 'to attribute', or
'to call something something'.

00:05:06.271 --> 00:05:10.319
Like when you say the
world exists just in words

00:05:10.319 --> 00:05:13.875
or exists in names, the verb is GDAGS.

00:05:13.875 --> 00:05:17.773
So anytime you see GDAGS,
usually at the end of a verse,

00:05:17.773 --> 00:05:22.773
it means, how can you speak of
that, how can you apply that,

00:05:25.350 --> 00:05:29.600
how can you refer to it
this way, meaning, falsely,

00:05:30.510 --> 00:05:32.687
often times. All right.

00:05:32.687 --> 00:05:35.281
So, 'GZHAN SDE NA RE'

00:05:35.281 --> 00:05:37.759
Here's the actual debate (claps)

00:05:37.759 --> 00:05:41.523
GZHAN SDE NA RE means 'other
traditions of ancient India',

00:05:41.523 --> 00:05:44.043
maybe the Sankhya, the Hindu tradition.

00:05:44.043 --> 00:05:47.267
They come to Nagarjuna and they say,

00:05:47.267 --> 00:05:49.819
DUS RANG BZHIN KYIS YOD DE,

00:05:49.819 --> 00:05:51.947
"Time exists in and of itself."

00:05:51.947 --> 00:05:54.123
It is not a creation of our mind.

00:05:54.123 --> 00:05:57.091
It not a creation of our karmic seeds.

00:05:57.091 --> 00:06:01.648
It's not even a creation of
comparison between the times.

00:06:01.648 --> 00:06:03.963
It exists in and of itself.

00:06:03.963 --> 00:06:07.296
Time exists in a certain measurable way,

00:06:08.299 --> 00:06:10.367
and it is not relative.

00:06:10.367 --> 00:06:14.191
So, you can say science
before Einstein, right?

00:06:14.191 --> 00:06:15.858
Newtonian mechanics.

00:06:16.951 --> 00:06:19.451
Time has a certain definition.

00:06:21.139 --> 00:06:22.701
RANG BZHIN KYIS YOD DE

00:06:22.701 --> 00:06:23.534
Why?

00:06:23.534 --> 00:06:26.850
SKAD CIG DANG THANG CIG DANG
YUD TZAM DANG NYIN MTSAN LA

00:06:26.850 --> 00:06:29.363
SOGS PA TSAD DON GZHAN
DANG LTAN {%LDAN?} PA'I PHYIR

00:06:29.363 --> 00:06:33.530
Because time can be measured
in agreed upon units.

00:06:35.451 --> 00:06:40.447
Time is measured in
seconds, minutes, hours,

00:06:40.447 --> 00:06:43.947
days, season, years, decades, centuries.

00:06:47.233 --> 00:06:50.435
We have created words and concepts

00:06:50.435 --> 00:06:52.955
for the measurement of time.

00:06:52.955 --> 00:06:56.313
So therefore, time does
have some existence

00:06:56.313 --> 00:06:59.915
from its own side or it
could not have been named

00:06:59.915 --> 00:07:03.667
millisecond, second, like that.

00:07:03.667 --> 00:07:07.927
These are real measurements that you know,

00:07:07.927 --> 00:07:12.927
an hour is 60 minutes. It's
not like 61, it's not like 59.

00:07:13.372 --> 00:07:16.634
Therefore, it has some inherent existence.

00:07:16.634 --> 00:07:18.432
An hour has to be 60 minutes.

00:07:18.432 --> 00:07:21.163
It's an agreed upon convention.

00:07:21.163 --> 00:07:26.129
Therefore, it has some
independent existence.

00:07:26.129 --> 00:07:28.231
And here's Nagarjuna's response

00:07:28.231 --> 00:07:32.011
DE MI RIGS TE

00:07:32.011 --> 00:07:36.087
That's MI RIGS, that's
BS, that's all wrong. Why?

00:07:36.087 --> 00:07:39.599
DUS NI SKAD CIG RE RE
BZHIN ZHIG NAS 'GRO BAS

00:07:39.599 --> 00:07:44.599
If you think about it, time
passes in fleeting milliseconds.

00:07:46.679 --> 00:07:49.929
ZHIG NAS 'GRO BAS THER ZUG MI GNAS

00:07:50.787 --> 00:07:53.756
THER ZUG is a beautiful
word you should learn, Tim.

00:07:53.756 --> 00:07:56.807
It means that 'it has no solid existence'.

00:07:56.807 --> 00:08:00.959
THER ZUG is a beautiful,
poetic word that means

00:08:00.959 --> 00:08:02.999
it has no solid existence.

00:08:02.999 --> 00:08:05.098
THER ZUG TU MI GNAS PA'I PHYIR DANG

00:08:05.098 --> 00:08:06.315
DUS LAS NGO BO THA DAD
PA'I SKAD CIG LA SOGS PA'I

00:08:06.315 --> 00:08:09.065
SGO NAS 'DZIN PAR MI BYED PA'I PHYIR,

00:08:10.329 --> 00:08:13.319
And you can't hold onto time.

00:08:13.319 --> 00:08:16.617
You cannot grasp time,
you cannot catch time

00:08:16.617 --> 00:08:20.117
and freeze it. Time is un-freezable.

00:08:21.800 --> 00:08:26.043
Time cannot be, you cannot
grasp a certain millisecond

00:08:26.043 --> 00:08:28.611
and say, "That's a millisecond."

00:08:28.611 --> 00:08:32.539
It's too fleeting, it's too, un-THER ZUG

00:08:32.539 --> 00:08:34.872
to even call it substantial.

00:08:36.319 --> 00:08:37.152
PHYI MA GRUB STE,

00:08:37.152 --> 00:08:37.985
DUS NI DE DAG CI RIGS LA BRTEN NAS

00:08:37.985 --> 00:08:38.818
'DOGS PA TZAM YIN PA'I PHYIR

00:08:46.642 --> 00:08:50.574
Units of time require
artificial measures.

00:08:50.574 --> 00:08:53.167
They require artificial concepts.

00:08:53.167 --> 00:08:56.407
You know, 60 minutes will be an hour.

00:08:56.407 --> 00:08:59.275
And that's all made up in your mind.

00:08:59.275 --> 00:09:04.275
You are taking things, all
units of time, up to centuries,

00:09:05.693 --> 00:09:08.799
ultimately depend on milliseconds

00:09:08.799 --> 00:09:11.492
or the shortest period of time.

00:09:11.492 --> 00:09:15.607
And those are so fleeting that
you cannot grasp onto them.

00:09:15.607 --> 00:09:19.267
So if you pile up ungraspable things,

00:09:19.267 --> 00:09:22.643
you end up with just a
big ungraspable thing.

00:09:22.643 --> 00:09:26.739
It's not like it becomes more
solid when you pile them up.

00:09:26.739 --> 00:09:29.695
Because each one is undefinable.

00:09:29.695 --> 00:09:34.695
Each one is uncatchable. You
can't catch a moment in time.

00:09:35.301 --> 00:09:38.711
When you turn 69 you'll
understand it better.

00:09:38.711 --> 00:09:41.378
(soft laughter)

00:09:42.495 --> 00:09:45.473
Then the other guy says
a very beautiful thing

00:09:45.473 --> 00:09:47.591
and these are all very thought-provoking,

00:09:47.591 --> 00:09:52.375
and if you have time, these
are great for conversation

00:09:52.375 --> 00:09:55.883
in a study group or for
meditation on your own.

00:09:55.883 --> 00:09:58.341
And the one here he says,

00:09:58.341 --> 00:09:59.174
KHO NA RE,

00:10:01.626 --> 00:10:05.973
Now here's another school's
thought and they say,

00:10:05.973 --> 00:10:07.956
DUS KYIS 'BYUNG BA SMIN PA

00:10:11.622 --> 00:10:12.455
Time... how are we going to say this...

00:10:16.539 --> 00:10:19.289
Time brings the elemental forces

00:10:20.373 --> 00:10:22.737
of the universe to fruition.

00:10:22.737 --> 00:10:24.793
Time brings the elemental forces

00:10:24.793 --> 00:10:28.009
of the the universe to fruition.

00:10:28.009 --> 00:10:29.926
Therefore, it is solid.

00:10:34.944 --> 00:10:39.927
Time's effect on gravity,
inertia, entropy,

00:10:41.887 --> 00:10:44.970
basic electromagnetism,

00:10:46.387 --> 00:10:49.987
time's effect on those
elemental forces of the universe

00:10:49.987 --> 00:10:53.915
demonstrate that time must
have some solid existence

00:10:53.915 --> 00:10:56.928
because it effects other things.

00:10:56.928 --> 00:11:01.399
Time causes...this morning they announced

00:11:01.399 --> 00:11:06.232
the astronomers for the first
time watched a star explode.

00:11:07.276 --> 00:11:10.472
In present time, meaning
the light reached us,

00:11:10.472 --> 00:11:12.424
they were able to observe a star exploding

00:11:12.424 --> 00:11:15.155
for the first time, in real time.

00:11:15.155 --> 00:11:17.887
I mean just a couple billion years behind.

00:11:17.887 --> 00:11:19.895
(laughs)

00:11:19.895 --> 00:11:23.555
What this guy says, what this
non-Buddhist guy says is,

00:11:23.555 --> 00:11:28.555
"Look, if time had no substantial
existence, if it weren't solid,

00:11:28.924 --> 00:11:32.855
then how can it cause a star to explode?"

00:11:32.855 --> 00:11:35.132
A star explodes over
certain amount of time.

00:11:35.132 --> 00:11:37.735
The time comes, it explodes,
it keeps exploding.

00:11:37.735 --> 00:11:40.491
This requires time. In a way you can,

00:11:40.491 --> 00:11:45.007
and it's going to come later,
time has a solid existence

00:11:45.007 --> 00:11:47.007
because it is tetherable

00:11:48.501 --> 00:11:51.084
to the phases of solid objects.

00:11:53.604 --> 00:11:57.187
So you can say that
the phases of the moon,

00:11:58.236 --> 00:12:02.828
you can assign them times.
And those are so solid,

00:12:02.828 --> 00:12:04.616
that the times you assign to them:

00:12:04.616 --> 00:12:08.872
full moon, half moon,
rising moon, dark moon,

00:12:08.872 --> 00:12:12.455
those must have some
solid existences also.

00:12:14.540 --> 00:12:18.968
The conventions that are
admittedly artificial of time,

00:12:18.968 --> 00:12:23.051
if they are linked to a
real, natural occurrence,

00:12:23.980 --> 00:12:26.092
like the phases of the moon,

00:12:26.092 --> 00:12:28.341
then they take on some real existence.

00:12:28.341 --> 00:12:29.980
They have some solid existence.

00:12:29.980 --> 00:12:32.730
They're tied to a thing changing.

00:12:38.255 --> 00:12:41.304
Then Nagarjuna won't have it.

00:12:41.304 --> 00:12:45.443
And he says, "No no no,
you're trying to point out",

00:12:45.443 --> 00:12:47.816
for example, a change in
the phase in the moon.

00:12:47.816 --> 00:12:50.784
"You're trying to say that a certain time",

00:12:50.784 --> 00:12:55.603
I don't know, "a half moon,
becomes a three-quarter moon

00:12:55.603 --> 00:12:59.156
"or something, you're making
an artificial boundary

00:12:59.156 --> 00:13:03.818
between a half moon and
a three-quarters moon."

00:13:03.818 --> 00:13:08.068
But if you think about it, the
moment in which a half moon

00:13:08.068 --> 00:13:11.548
changes to a three-quarter
moon is not something

00:13:11.548 --> 00:13:14.548
you can freeze. You can't freeze it.

00:13:16.390 --> 00:13:19.468
There's no point at which
it is purely half moon

00:13:19.468 --> 00:13:21.088
and then in the next moment

00:13:21.088 --> 00:13:22.856
it is purely three-quarters moon.

00:13:22.856 --> 00:13:24.808
There's always some kind of overlap.

00:13:24.808 --> 00:13:27.252
We call it phases of the moon.

00:13:27.252 --> 00:13:30.012
It phases into the three-quarter moon.

00:13:30.012 --> 00:13:33.160
There's no solid line where you can say,

00:13:33.160 --> 00:13:35.007
it's now the three quarter moon.

00:13:35.007 --> 00:13:37.916
You could say, you look it up online

00:13:37.916 --> 00:13:42.549
and it says the moon's going
to change phase at 3:05 today.

00:13:42.549 --> 00:13:46.588
But which 3:05? 3:05 and a
half? 3:05 and a quarter?

00:13:46.588 --> 00:13:48.864
And then which 3:05 and a quarter?

00:13:48.864 --> 00:13:50.396
The first part of 3:05 and a quarter

00:13:50.396 --> 00:13:52.719
or the second part of 3:05 and a quarter?

00:13:52.719 --> 00:13:55.360
And it's all...it is not something,

00:13:55.360 --> 00:13:58.152
they keep using the word GZUNG.

00:13:58.152 --> 00:14:02.569
Which means 'DZIN, which
means pointable to, grabbable.

00:14:04.288 --> 00:14:06.580
Definable. Can't say this is the moment

00:14:06.580 --> 00:14:08.747
at which the moon changed.

00:14:10.254 --> 00:14:12.340
SKAD CIG DANG THANG CIG LA

00:14:12.340 --> 00:14:13.840
And by the way,

00:14:15.786 --> 00:14:20.703
in Buddhist philosophy, what's
the definition of existence?

00:14:22.600 --> 00:14:25.692
[Student] That which is
perceived by valid perception

00:14:25.692 --> 00:14:26.788
Yes.

00:14:26.788 --> 00:14:28.812
TSAD MAS DMIGS PA YOD PA'I MTSAN NYID

00:14:28.812 --> 00:14:31.016
Anything which is perceived

00:14:31.016 --> 00:14:33.880
by an accurate perception exists.

00:14:33.880 --> 00:14:37.032
And if it cannot be perceived
by an accurate perception

00:14:37.032 --> 00:14:38.260
it doesn't exist.

00:14:38.260 --> 00:14:40.059
What about a tree that falls in the forest

00:14:40.059 --> 00:14:41.936
and nobody's there?
Somebody's always there

00:14:41.936 --> 00:14:44.860
because the Buddha can see everything.

00:14:44.860 --> 00:14:47.472
That's the classic answer.

00:14:47.472 --> 00:14:49.472
But, TSAD MAS DMIGS PA means,

00:14:51.361 --> 00:14:55.361
can an accurate perception
identify that moment?

00:14:57.868 --> 00:15:02.066
And Nagarjuna says, "No.
Because identify means grasp."

00:15:02.066 --> 00:15:06.840
Identify means grab onto a
certain point at which it is

00:15:06.840 --> 00:15:10.993
the millisecond. But you
can't, there's no such thing.

00:15:10.993 --> 00:15:15.243
Therefore, if you can't
grab, if you can't identify

00:15:16.244 --> 00:15:19.947
the front end and back
end of a millisecond,

00:15:19.947 --> 00:15:23.536
if you cannot identify the
front end and the back end

00:15:23.536 --> 00:15:24.659
of a millisecond,

00:15:24.659 --> 00:15:28.181
then because of the definition
of existence in Buddhism,

00:15:28.181 --> 00:15:30.431
a millisecond cannot exist.

00:15:31.560 --> 00:15:34.251
Because there is no pramana,

00:15:34.251 --> 00:15:35.196
there is no accurate perception

00:15:35.196 --> 00:15:37.348
which can catch it.

00:15:37.348 --> 00:15:40.015
Maybe catch is a good verb here.

00:15:41.184 --> 00:15:44.668
And that's a cool point.
That's a very cool point.

00:15:44.668 --> 00:15:45.501
DE LTAR MA BZUNG BA'I DUS DE NI
SKAD CIG LA SOGS PA'I TSAD KYIS

00:15:45.501 --> 00:15:46.334
JI LTAR GDAGS PAR YANG
MI NUS TE, MA 'BREL BA'I PHYIR

00:15:52.838 --> 00:15:57.838
And if you can't even identify
a single millisecond of time,

00:15:58.572 --> 00:16:02.188
then how are you going to describe

00:16:02.188 --> 00:16:05.216
the difference between past
and present and future?

00:16:05.216 --> 00:16:09.248
Because that hinges upon a
moment in which things change

00:16:09.248 --> 00:16:14.248
to present from past, or
change from present to future.

00:16:14.592 --> 00:16:19.164
If there's no point at which
past changes to present,

00:16:19.164 --> 00:16:21.713
and if present never makes
contact with the past,

00:16:21.713 --> 00:16:25.212
if there's no point of
contact which is observable,

00:16:25.212 --> 00:16:27.713
then the divisions of time,

00:16:27.713 --> 00:16:31.392
even into past, present,
and future are artificial

00:16:31.392 --> 00:16:34.480
and arbitrary, and they
don't really exist,

00:16:34.480 --> 00:16:37.147
from their own side. Cool.

00:16:41.546 --> 00:16:42.824
I was trying to get a picture.

00:16:42.824 --> 00:16:45.300
I really love finding these pictures.

00:16:45.300 --> 00:16:48.404
It's like I spend all day
doing it. My wife wonders

00:16:48.404 --> 00:16:49.654
what I'm doing.

00:16:53.444 --> 00:16:57.824
I found a picture for
this one. I like this one.

00:16:57.824 --> 00:16:59.657
Time has just flown...

00:17:02.272 --> 00:17:04.939
Time flies out of the birdcage.

00:17:06.264 --> 00:17:08.768
And you can't grab it

00:17:08.768 --> 00:17:11.272
and say, "That's the
present or that's the past,

00:17:11.272 --> 00:17:14.196
or that's when present
shifted to the past."

00:17:14.196 --> 00:17:15.496
It's un-grabbable.

00:17:15.496 --> 00:17:18.601
You grasp and it's gone already.

00:17:18.601 --> 00:17:21.434
So I enjoy finding these pictures.

00:17:22.576 --> 00:17:25.928
Now we reached the second
part. What was that?

00:17:25.928 --> 00:17:30.244
We discussed other
traditions of ancient India.

00:17:30.244 --> 00:17:35.244
Their idea of time, and
what did he promise us next?

00:17:35.402 --> 00:17:37.274
Yes, how about some of our own groups,

00:17:37.274 --> 00:17:39.986
who are not Middle Way?

00:17:39.986 --> 00:17:42.559
What about the first three groups?

00:17:42.559 --> 00:17:44.478
He's going to treat the
first three groups together.

00:17:44.478 --> 00:17:48.157
Abhidharma, Sutrist and Mind Only,

00:17:48.157 --> 00:17:50.030
which are called the functionalists.

00:17:50.030 --> 00:17:51.758
They all believe that something is real

00:17:51.758 --> 00:17:55.150
if it exhibits some kind of function.

00:17:55.150 --> 00:17:58.013
So he says, "How do they think about time?

00:17:58.013 --> 00:18:01.520
And can we poke holes in
their idea about time?"

00:18:01.520 --> 00:18:04.639
Which is pretty much what
Nagarjuna spends his time doing,

00:18:04.639 --> 00:18:06.806
if there were such a time.

00:18:09.176 --> 00:18:10.426
So, here we go.

00:18:11.790 --> 00:18:14.540
And by the way just for you guys,

00:18:17.396 --> 00:18:21.563
the question of time is a
very unique circumstance

00:18:24.430 --> 00:18:28.680
where the fourth school
and the first school agree,

00:18:30.028 --> 00:18:32.611
and the second and third don't.

00:18:34.990 --> 00:18:39.085
The idea that time is a functioning thing,

00:18:39.085 --> 00:18:41.932
or the past especially, or the future.

00:18:41.932 --> 00:18:46.476
The idea that the past does
something is not accepted

00:18:46.476 --> 00:18:48.191
by school number two,

00:18:48.191 --> 00:18:50.684
is not accepted by school number three,

00:18:50.684 --> 00:18:53.851
but it is accepted by the
Abhidharma and Middle Way.

00:18:53.851 --> 00:18:57.373
So it's a very rare case
where Abhidharma agrees

00:18:57.373 --> 00:18:59.453
with Middle Way.

00:18:59.453 --> 00:19:01.513
Now, and then what you have
to say in the next breath

00:19:01.513 --> 00:19:04.263
always is, for different reasons.

00:19:05.454 --> 00:19:07.204
Anyway, cancel that.

00:19:08.287 --> 00:19:09.204
Here we go.

00:19:11.756 --> 00:19:13.914
,GAL TE DUS NI DNGOS RTEN TE,

00:19:13.914 --> 00:19:15.822
,DNGOS MED DUS NI GA LA YOD,

00:19:15.822 --> 00:19:17.630
,DNGOS PO 'GA' YANG YOD MA YIN,

00:19:17.630 --> 00:19:19.846
,DUS LTA YOD PAR GA LA 'GYUR,

00:19:22.652 --> 00:19:25.235
And let's go to the commentary.

00:19:28.191 --> 00:19:30.349
GAL TE RTAG PA'I DUS MED KYANG
SKAD CIG SOGS LA BRTEN NAS

00:19:30.349 --> 00:19:33.998
THA DAD DU BZHAG PA'I DUS
NI NGO BOS YOD DO ZHE NA,

00:19:33.998 --> 00:19:38.943
Somebody says, Look, I agree
there's no such thing as time

00:19:38.943 --> 00:19:43.360
which is unchanging. That
far I agree with you.

00:19:44.307 --> 00:19:45.140
But...

00:19:46.772 --> 00:19:49.105
you can posit separate times

00:19:50.956 --> 00:19:53.373
like past present and future

00:19:55.372 --> 00:19:59.882
based on instances. You can
use the concept of a second

00:19:59.882 --> 00:20:02.732
or an instant, and based on that,

00:20:02.732 --> 00:20:05.965
you can artificially divide
the past from the present

00:20:05.965 --> 00:20:08.652
and the present from the future.

00:20:08.652 --> 00:20:11.235
If we all agree that we can use

00:20:12.156 --> 00:20:15.438
an idea called a second or an hour,

00:20:15.438 --> 00:20:16.987
then we can divide things.

00:20:16.987 --> 00:20:20.477
This happened at 3 o'clock.
this happened at 4 o'clock.

00:20:20.477 --> 00:20:24.142
We can, using artificial measurements

00:20:24.142 --> 00:20:26.601
created by human beings,

00:20:26.601 --> 00:20:30.184
we can describe time
this way. That's okay.

00:20:32.156 --> 00:20:35.814
This is a lower three schools idea.

00:20:35.814 --> 00:20:39.022
GAL TE GZUGS LA SOGS PA'I DNGOS PO
MED PA STE DE DAG LAS NGO BO 

00:20:39.022 --> 00:20:41.588
THA DAD PA'I DUS NI GA LA
YOD DE, DUS NI DNGOS PO LA

00:20:41.588 --> 00:20:44.363
BRTEN NAS 'JOG PA'I PHYIR,

00:20:49.512 --> 00:20:53.512
Then he says, "Does the
artificial units of time

00:20:56.415 --> 00:21:00.415
which you agree to use, seconds, minutes?"

00:21:01.517 --> 00:21:05.232
And this is cute, Nagarjuna
says, "Okay you want to make up

00:21:05.232 --> 00:21:07.536
artificial measurements of time, right?"

00:21:07.536 --> 00:21:09.309
And the guy says, "Yeah let's do that."

00:21:09.309 --> 00:21:11.022
And like, "What do you want to use?"

00:21:11.022 --> 00:21:13.087
He says, "Well, let's
use minutes and hours

00:21:13.087 --> 00:21:15.263
and how about that?"

00:21:15.263 --> 00:21:17.055
And this is another school and he says,

00:21:17.055 --> 00:21:20.285
"Yeah, but I'll tell you something..."

00:21:20.285 --> 00:21:22.158
Now, you guys got to cook it,

00:21:22.158 --> 00:21:25.102
you guys got to see if you agree on it.

00:21:25.102 --> 00:21:29.352
But Nagarjuna says, "Those
artificial units of time

00:21:30.504 --> 00:21:35.504
that you just make up and we
all agree to use by convention

00:21:35.709 --> 00:21:39.342
by mutual convention,
they exist only relative

00:21:39.342 --> 00:21:41.425
to an observable entity."

00:21:44.285 --> 00:21:48.702
You can only say minutes,
hours and days relative to

00:21:49.695 --> 00:21:53.370
the moon phases changing or
the time it takes the sun

00:21:53.370 --> 00:21:55.417
to come up and go down.

00:21:55.417 --> 00:21:59.834
It's only by relative to
observable, actual entities,

00:22:01.454 --> 00:22:04.765
that you could have an
artificial entity called hour

00:22:04.765 --> 00:22:07.952
or day or month. Month as you know

00:22:07.952 --> 00:22:10.128
is based on the phases of the moon.

00:22:10.128 --> 00:22:13.072
Day is based on the phases of the sun.

00:22:13.072 --> 00:22:16.447
And in Buddhist cosmology,
there are other measurements

00:22:16.447 --> 00:22:18.990
of time based on the movement
of the constellations.

00:22:18.990 --> 00:22:22.323
So in Buddhist cosmology, lunar, solar

00:22:23.862 --> 00:22:27.695
and what is it, stellar,
measurement of time.

00:22:28.944 --> 00:22:33.944
But, all three choices depend
on an outside physical entity.

00:22:34.926 --> 00:22:37.712
The moon, the sun, the stars.

00:22:37.712 --> 00:22:41.296
So Nagarjuna says, "Look, I agree,

00:22:41.296 --> 00:22:45.213
you can make up units of
time. But don't you always

00:22:45.213 --> 00:22:49.565
tie them to some
observable outside thing?"

00:22:49.565 --> 00:22:54.106
Like a season is kind of tied
to the change in the weather,

00:22:54.106 --> 00:22:55.598
and things like that.

00:22:55.598 --> 00:22:57.195
So he says, "Don't they always?"

00:22:57.195 --> 00:23:01.200
And then the other guys like,
"Yes, now that you mention it

00:23:01.200 --> 00:23:04.491
the artificial units of
time which we have created

00:23:04.491 --> 00:23:06.158
are created relative to,

00:23:06.158 --> 00:23:09.136
they have to be linked
to, a changing DNGOS PO,

00:23:09.136 --> 00:23:11.685
a functioning entity, on the outside."

00:23:11.685 --> 00:23:14.252
DNGOS PO 'GA' YANG RANG BZHIN GYIS
YOD PA MIN NA DE LA BTAGS PA'I

00:23:14.252 --> 00:23:15.305
DUS LTA

00:23:15.305 --> 00:23:17.532
And then he says, "Well," says Nagarjuna,

00:23:17.532 --> 00:23:21.488
"since the outside observable entities

00:23:21.488 --> 00:23:24.351
that you want to link time
to don't exist from their own

00:23:24.351 --> 00:23:29.351
side, then neither does the
time that is linked to them."

00:23:29.407 --> 00:23:32.636
If the moon doesn't
exist from it's own side,

00:23:32.636 --> 00:23:37.636
then the time, the artificial
convention called month

00:23:37.723 --> 00:23:40.701
that you linked to that
thing, cannot be real.

00:23:40.701 --> 00:23:45.034
Because the thing it is a
characteristic of is not real.

00:23:47.007 --> 00:23:51.197
If the jar is not real,
then the green color

00:23:51.197 --> 00:23:53.928
of the jar is not real.

00:23:53.928 --> 00:23:58.049
If the moon is not real, then
the artificial measurement

00:23:58.049 --> 00:24:01.726
month, based on the moon,
has to share that quality,

00:24:01.726 --> 00:24:04.255
and it's not real either.

00:24:04.255 --> 00:24:06.975
So that requires Nagarjuna to prove

00:24:06.975 --> 00:24:09.296
that the moon is not real. (laughs)

00:24:09.296 --> 00:24:10.732
So that's another story.

00:24:10.732 --> 00:24:13.648
But he says, "Can we agree in theory,

00:24:13.648 --> 00:24:15.982
that if the moon is not real,

00:24:15.982 --> 00:24:19.374
then the artificial measurement
month, that you create

00:24:19.374 --> 00:24:24.374
based upon the movement of
the moon is also not real?"

00:24:24.463 --> 00:24:28.189
If you're creating a
convention that relies upon

00:24:28.189 --> 00:24:30.369
another object's activity,

00:24:30.369 --> 00:24:32.575
then if the other object's activity

00:24:32.575 --> 00:24:35.037
is not coming from its own side,

00:24:35.037 --> 00:24:37.584
if it's a fabrication of your mind,

00:24:37.584 --> 00:24:40.622
due to your karmic seeds, then

00:24:40.622 --> 00:24:43.199
can't we say time has to be that way also,

00:24:43.199 --> 00:24:46.591
if it's a feature of that object?

00:24:46.591 --> 00:24:48.656
If time is a feature of the moon

00:24:48.656 --> 00:24:50.654
and the moon is not coming from the moon,

00:24:50.654 --> 00:24:54.480
then can't we say that the
time that is based on the moon

00:24:54.480 --> 00:24:57.068
has to be coming from you also?

00:24:57.068 --> 00:25:00.136
And that it doesn't
actually have a measure,

00:25:00.136 --> 00:25:02.128
an independent measure.

00:25:02.128 --> 00:25:03.795
So it doesn't exist.

00:25:04.778 --> 00:25:07.722
And moreover....(chuckles)

00:25:07.722 --> 00:25:12.722
is there a point of contact
between the moon and month?

00:25:12.940 --> 00:25:16.345
Months depend on the
movement of the moon. Yes.

00:25:16.345 --> 00:25:19.705
Is there relationship
between them or not? Yes.

00:25:19.705 --> 00:25:24.123
Is the idea month connected
to the moon? Yes.

00:25:24.123 --> 00:25:27.515
Is there a point of contact? Yes,

00:25:27.515 --> 00:25:30.843
otherwise month would be
connected to solar activity

00:25:30.843 --> 00:25:34.616
or stellar activity. There
has to be a point of contact.

00:25:34.616 --> 00:25:36.154
Well what part of the moon

00:25:36.154 --> 00:25:38.220
touches what part of month, please?

00:25:38.220 --> 00:25:40.332
Like that, it's impossible.

00:25:40.332 --> 00:25:43.020
Month cannot be tied to the moon.

00:25:43.020 --> 00:25:46.617
Because moon itself is
not tied to anything.

00:25:46.617 --> 00:25:48.284
It's very beautiful.

00:25:49.740 --> 00:25:51.740
You got to cook that one.

00:25:55.065 --> 00:25:57.315
Then, by the way, 

00:25:58.198 --> 00:26:00.793
Can you see the sentence
DUS GSUM GYI RNAM GZHAG?

00:26:00.793 --> 00:26:04.104
Do you see a sentence starting
DUS GSUM GYI RNAM GZHAG?

00:26:04.104 --> 00:26:05.854
Can you display that?

00:26:15.183 --> 00:26:18.435
I don't know I didn't check the colophon,

00:26:18.435 --> 00:26:20.803
which is the last few
sentences of the book

00:26:20.803 --> 00:26:23.753
where he says, "I wrote this in my house,

00:26:23.753 --> 00:26:26.170
in Choney Monastery, in 1747."

00:26:28.729 --> 00:26:32.646
But I'm guessing it was late in his life.

00:26:32.646 --> 00:26:36.086
He had a spurt of activity
late in his life, Choney Lama.

00:26:36.086 --> 00:26:38.504
He wrote 200 books, I think half of them

00:26:38.504 --> 00:26:40.293
were in the last ten years.

00:26:40.293 --> 00:26:43.223
He went into retreat and
said, "I got to get this down!

00:26:43.223 --> 00:26:44.681
I got to get this down!

00:26:44.681 --> 00:26:46.867
They're going to mess it up after me."

00:26:46.867 --> 00:26:48.087
I know the feeling.

00:26:48.087 --> 00:26:51.031
So he's working like an insane man.

00:26:51.031 --> 00:26:54.521
And he's cranking out these
books because he's thinking,

00:26:54.521 --> 00:26:56.773
"no one's going to be
able to do it after me."

00:26:56.773 --> 00:27:01.773
And then you get frustrating
sentences like this one, Tim.

00:27:01.800 --> 00:27:06.800
DUS GSUM GYI RNAM GZHAG
This whole thing about

00:27:06.965 --> 00:27:09.143
RNAM GZHAG means this whole presentation

00:27:09.143 --> 00:27:10.952
on the idea of the three times.

00:27:10.952 --> 00:27:14.310
BDAG GIS 'JUG PA'I RNAM BSHAD

00:27:14.310 --> 00:27:17.424
I covered the whole
thing in my commentary to

00:27:17.424 --> 00:27:20.898
DBU MA LA JUG PA, Madhyamakavatara,

00:27:21.798 --> 00:27:25.881
"Entering the Middle Way" by Chandrakirti.
480
00:27:25,881 --&gt; 00:27:28,423s
He says, "Hey, go and check
my commentary to Chadrakirti,

00:27:28.423 --> 00:27:31.006
"not my commentary to Nagarjuna.

00:27:32.025 --> 00:27:33.463
"I already wrote the whole thing,

00:27:33.463 --> 00:27:36.343
"I already did the whole
thing. You want more on this?

00:27:36.343 --> 00:27:38.839
"Go read my other book."

00:27:38.839 --> 00:27:41.190
It's kind of frustrating when
you're translating this book.

00:27:41.190 --> 00:27:43.107
I believe that morally

00:27:45.587 --> 00:27:47.924
we will have to go get it,

00:27:47.924 --> 00:27:51.218
snip it out, and put it in an appendix.

00:27:51.218 --> 00:27:52.661
Then we'll have to translate it again

00:27:52.661 --> 00:27:55.814
when we translate that book. But anyway...

00:27:55.814 --> 00:27:58.003
So that's what I always do, personally I,

00:27:58.003 --> 00:28:02.564
if he says, "I already covered
this is my other book,"

00:28:02.564 --> 00:28:06.950
I feel obligated to go
find it, cut it out,

00:28:06.950 --> 00:28:09.442
put it in the appendix and translate it.

00:28:09.442 --> 00:28:12.966
We can only do that because
we have this amazing database,

00:28:12.966 --> 00:28:17.383
created by Grandpa John.
Ot's the Grandpa #2.

00:28:18.283 --> 00:28:20.283
Grandpa John Brady. Yay.

00:28:21.221 --> 00:28:25.510
And it's so easy. It's so delightful to,

00:28:25.510 --> 00:28:30.282
you know, in the old days it
would have taken many days.

00:28:30.282 --> 00:28:34.782
But nowadays, you just sit
there, at my kitchen table,

00:28:35.941 --> 00:28:40.918
and I say, "Hey database, tell
me what did Choney Lama say

00:28:40.918 --> 00:28:43.735
about the three times in
that other commentary?"

00:28:43.735 --> 00:28:47.016
And you can find it in about 30 seconds.

00:28:47.016 --> 00:28:50.839
And you can cut it out
in another five seconds.

00:28:50.839 --> 00:28:54.423
Then you have to translate.
That takes the same time.

00:28:54.423 --> 00:28:57.384
But it's very cool.
It's unbelievably cool.

00:28:57.384 --> 00:29:00.392
That's why these Mixed
Nuts books are so valuable,

00:29:00.392 --> 00:29:01.559
in my opinion.

00:29:06.670 --> 00:29:11.434
I have an example, I don't
know, I was trying to get cute,

00:29:11.434 --> 00:29:13.934
which I'm always trying to do.

00:29:16.234 --> 00:29:18.794
And there's a book and a clock.

00:29:18.794 --> 00:29:20.127
And the idea is

00:29:22.519 --> 00:29:27.042
only relative to an outside
object does time have meaning.

00:29:27.042 --> 00:29:29.738
So how old is the book?
When was the book written?

00:29:29.738 --> 00:29:32.394
How many years until the
Mixed Nuts translate it?

00:29:32.394 --> 00:29:35.114
And Jasso becomes a Mixed Nut and translates?

00:29:35.114 --> 00:29:38.031
How many years will pass and those,

00:29:39.756 --> 00:29:42.409
the measurement of year only has meaning

00:29:42.409 --> 00:29:45.371
relative to the physical book.

00:29:45.371 --> 00:29:48.204
The book is defining time in a way.

00:29:48.204 --> 00:29:51.817
The phases of the book's
existence are defining time.

00:29:51.817 --> 00:29:53.944
When did John Brady input it?

00:29:53.944 --> 00:29:55.445
When was it published?

00:29:55.445 --> 00:29:58.713
When was it put online for free?

00:29:58.713 --> 00:30:02.296
Those are all created measurements of time.

00:30:04.780 --> 00:30:05.613
Part two.

00:30:08.313 --> 00:30:11.313
Refer to the Elephant's Dance Sutra.

00:30:16.791 --> 00:30:18.847
Any guesses what that means?

00:30:18.847 --> 00:30:19.921
(student murmuring)

00:30:19.921 --> 00:30:23.854
We said every chapter he's going to say,

00:30:23.854 --> 00:30:27.262
"Hey, here's where
Nagarjuna got these ideas."

00:30:27.262 --> 00:30:29.990
He's going to point back to the Buddha.

00:30:29.990 --> 00:30:31.339
He says, "You want to know where Nagarjuna

00:30:31.339 --> 00:30:33.484
"came up with all these time ideas?

00:30:33.484 --> 00:30:36.655
"Go check out the Elephant's Dance Sutra."

00:30:36.655 --> 00:30:38.075
That's Choney Llama.

00:30:38.075 --> 00:30:41.246
And I like it because it
means Nagarjuna's ideas

00:30:41.246 --> 00:30:45.470
are not just made up. The
ideas you are reading here,

00:30:45.470 --> 00:30:49.291
from Nagarjuna, are his interpretation

00:30:49.291 --> 00:30:53.388
of the original sutras. And
they always tie back to a sutra.

00:30:53.388 --> 00:30:58.140
There might be a clue there
in which sutras are tied to

00:30:58.140 --> 00:31:01.644
in working out the order
of chapters, but...

00:31:01.644 --> 00:31:03.020
that's too much.

00:31:03.020 --> 00:31:06.716
What's the third category
of this chapter going to be?

00:31:06.716 --> 00:31:07.883
Last sentence.

00:31:08.911 --> 00:31:10.575
The title of the chapter.

00:31:10.575 --> 00:31:13.083
Here we get in Asian languages,

00:31:13.083 --> 00:31:15.375
the name of the chapter comes at the end.

00:31:15.375 --> 00:31:17.150
DUS BRTAG PA

00:31:17.150 --> 00:31:20.619
kalā parīkṣā

00:31:20.619 --> 00:31:21.519
(student murmurs)

00:31:21.519 --> 00:31:23.980
An examination of time
or an investigation.

00:31:23.980 --> 00:31:25.356
I almost like investigation,
because Nagarjuna

00:31:25.356 --> 00:31:30.142
is kind of a very over,
what do you call it?

00:31:30.142 --> 00:31:33.854
Over-achiever guy. He's
going to investigate.

00:31:33.854 --> 00:31:37.804
He's not going to examine.
He's going to dig it out.

00:31:37.804 --> 00:31:41.132
I called them examinations for couple years

00:31:41.132 --> 00:31:43.727
and then I thought of investigation.

00:31:43.727 --> 00:31:47.854
Drop the examinations. Let's
go to the investigations.

00:31:47.854 --> 00:31:49.739
All right, next chapter.

00:31:49.739 --> 00:31:50.572
Hmm....

00:31:52.465 --> 00:31:54.465
What time, til 3:50 Tim?

00:31:55.311 --> 00:31:56.843
15 more minutes.

00:31:56.843 --> 00:31:57.979
Let's get here.

00:31:57.979 --> 00:32:02.396
Artificially, oh wait, I
shouldn't give you that.

00:32:03.695 --> 00:32:07.502
Anyway, Choney Llama attempts
to link this chapter

00:32:07.502 --> 00:32:10.877
to the last chapter. Chapter 20 is called

00:32:10.877 --> 00:32:15.104
"The Convergence of Causes and Conditions."

00:32:15.104 --> 00:32:17.088
And, let me see, I think
there's a Sanskrit here

00:32:17.088 --> 00:32:20.755
that's very useful.
Hmm....no not yet. Okay.

00:32:22.428 --> 00:32:26.496
Anyway there's this
beautiful concept in...

00:32:26.496 --> 00:32:29.235
(coughs) Sorry.

00:32:29.235 --> 00:32:33.902
In this, this chapter called,
I think it's called 'sāmagryā'.

00:32:36.750 --> 00:32:38.579
And you got to, I want
you to get the feeling

00:32:38.579 --> 00:32:40.098
before you jump into this chapter.

00:32:40.098 --> 00:32:44.415
I want you to get the feeling
of what he's talking about.

00:32:44.415 --> 00:32:47.832
He's talking about the time being so ripe

00:32:49.030 --> 00:32:52.068
that the thing has to happen.

00:32:52.068 --> 00:32:55.157
All the conditions come together.

00:32:55.157 --> 00:32:58.421
And you know, try to get your mind

00:32:58.421 --> 00:33:00.671
on that particular instant.

00:33:01.520 --> 00:33:06.520
All the necessary, the
e-book guy sent in his thing.

00:33:06.540 --> 00:33:10.207
You know, the app guy
just finished the app.

00:33:12.927 --> 00:33:14.507
Rosa finally has time to sit down

00:33:14.507 --> 00:33:17.227
for the first half of
the day and work on it.

00:33:17.227 --> 00:33:21.613
All these causes are going
to spit out a result.

00:33:21.613 --> 00:33:25.338
So, Choney Lama, Nagarjuna,
they want you to

00:33:25.338 --> 00:33:29.338
try to bring to mind that
special moment in time

00:33:31.181 --> 00:33:34.352
when all the necessary
causes and conditions

00:33:34.352 --> 00:33:37.260
have come together:

00:33:37.260 --> 00:33:42.260
finance, concept, accounting
department, human resources,

00:33:42.544 --> 00:33:45.598
blah blah blah. And now the
company can launch, you know?

00:33:45.598 --> 00:33:49.681
Like it's that moment just
before the cafe opens:

00:33:50.701 --> 00:33:54.188
the kitchen's done, the floor
is there, furniture is bought,

00:33:54.188 --> 00:33:55.774
the signs are made,

00:33:55.774 --> 00:33:57.857
the garden is landscaped,

00:33:59.324 --> 00:34:02.640
the toilet is there. (laughs)

00:34:02.640 --> 00:34:05.532
And boom, it's just about to open.

00:34:05.532 --> 00:34:08.063
He wants you to think about that moment.

00:34:08.063 --> 00:34:09.821
And he calls the chapter,

00:34:09.821 --> 00:34:12.479
the moment when all the
stuff came together.

00:34:12.479 --> 00:34:17.479
And it's the moment just
before the result pops up.

00:34:17.855 --> 00:34:20.855
All right, so get your mind on that.

00:34:32.143 --> 00:34:36.403
Now, here's the structure
of the whole chapter.

00:34:36.403 --> 00:34:40.820
You ready? Here's the
structure of the whole chapter.

00:34:42.035 --> 00:34:45.392
Try to put your mind
on that special moment

00:34:45.392 --> 00:34:48.575
when everything you need
for something to happen,

00:34:48.575 --> 00:34:52.083
like the opening of the cafe,
has finally come together.

00:34:52.083 --> 00:34:54.259
Everything's finished: furniture's bought,

00:34:54.259 --> 00:34:56.842
we found workers, all the
stuff has come together.

00:35:02.193 --> 00:35:04.307
Put your mind on that moment

00:35:04.307 --> 00:35:08.140
called the...what did I
call it the other day?

00:35:09.510 --> 00:35:11.923
The consummate completion.

00:35:11.923 --> 00:35:15.090
The consummate...coming together...consummate convergence.

00:35:17.008 --> 00:35:20.400
All the causes, all the necessary stuff

00:35:20.400 --> 00:35:23.966
that needed to happen has happened.

00:35:23.966 --> 00:35:25.966
Now, my question to you.

00:35:27.394 --> 00:35:31.144
Does that happen before
the result comes out?

00:35:32.095 --> 00:35:36.306
Does that happen as the result comes out?

00:35:36.306 --> 00:35:40.973
Or does it happen strangely
after the result comes out?

00:35:42.370 --> 00:35:46.559
Do you open the cafe,
after everything's ready?

00:35:46.559 --> 00:35:50.818
Or do you open the cafe just
before everything's ready?

00:35:50.818 --> 00:35:54.651
Or do you open the cafe
as everything's ready?

00:35:55.955 --> 00:35:58.128
I think naturally, what would we say?

00:35:58.128 --> 00:35:59.679
What do you guys think?

00:35:59.679 --> 00:36:00.512
(student replies): After everything's ready. 

00:36:00.512 --> 00:36:02.434
Yes, normally you'd say, "Geshe-la,

00:36:02.434 --> 00:36:05.570
get the money together
before you open it."

00:36:05.570 --> 00:36:07.879
Don't open it and then look for the money,

00:36:07.879 --> 00:36:08.986
nothing like that.

00:36:08.986 --> 00:36:11.519
By the way there is money.

00:36:11.519 --> 00:36:15.269
But I took the Doctor
Chen's advice long ago.

00:36:16.472 --> 00:36:21.139
30 years ago. I asked this
great Chinese philanthropist,

00:36:22.955 --> 00:36:25.383
"What's your secret for doing projects?"

00:36:25.383 --> 00:36:27.717
He says, "I start the project, Michael,

00:36:27.717 --> 00:36:32.457
after I have the money." (laughs)

00:36:32.457 --> 00:36:33.656
And I'll never forget it.

00:36:33.656 --> 00:36:36.739
I always...isn't that the story here?

00:36:39.125 --> 00:36:43.518
Start the cafe, open the cafe, after you got
the, what do you call that..

00:36:43.518 --> 00:36:47.277
capital together and don't mess with it.

00:36:47.277 --> 00:36:50.714
But, Nagarjuna requires us to consider

00:36:50.714 --> 00:36:52.682
the other two possibilities.

00:36:52.682 --> 00:36:55.038
Could it be possible that
the result is happening

00:36:55.038 --> 00:36:58.765
as the cause and the
conditions come together?

00:36:58.765 --> 00:37:01.182
Or could there be some angle

00:37:03.025 --> 00:37:05.406
where the causes and conditions
have only come together

00:37:05.406 --> 00:37:08.128
after the result has come?

00:37:08.128 --> 00:37:10.351
Could there be some way
of looking at it that way?

00:37:10.351 --> 00:37:12.239
Jasso's already there.

00:37:12.239 --> 00:37:16.989
Shh..don't tell on me...I can
sense you're already there.

00:37:17.837 --> 00:37:18.920
Here we go...

00:37:23.772 --> 00:37:26.939
Now a question. Put your mind on this.

00:37:30.684 --> 00:37:32.647
When you get all the money for the cafe

00:37:32.647 --> 00:37:35.659
and all the equipment
and all the employees,

00:37:35.659 --> 00:37:39.659
and blah blah blah...permits.
God bless permits.

00:37:41.278 --> 00:37:43.445
When they're all together,

00:37:45.724 --> 00:37:49.224
is the opening of the cafe present or not?

00:37:51.486 --> 00:37:56.069
In the convening of the
necessary causes and conditions

00:37:58.110 --> 00:37:59.860
is the opening in it?

00:38:01.071 --> 00:38:04.126
In the convention. Convening.

00:38:04.126 --> 00:38:06.793
Is the opening present in the

00:38:08.414 --> 00:38:13.180
final collection of all the
things you need to open?

00:38:13.180 --> 00:38:15.885
Is the opening there or not?

00:38:15.885 --> 00:38:18.526
I think conventionally
you'd say, "Not yet."

00:38:18.526 --> 00:38:21.051
Or, "It's just about to
be there," or something.

00:38:21.051 --> 00:38:23.260
The opening's not there,
but everything you need

00:38:23.260 --> 00:38:25.357
for the opening is there.

00:38:25.357 --> 00:38:27.867
But Geshe-la, technically, the opening

00:38:27.867 --> 00:38:30.575
is not one of the
conditions for the opening.

00:38:30.575 --> 00:38:33.071
So it's not there yet.

00:38:33.071 --> 00:38:34.349
Okay, got it?

00:38:34.349 --> 00:38:37.103
Like you can have a seed and
a gardener and a sunlight

00:38:37.103 --> 00:38:40.124
and fertilizer, but there's no flower yet.

00:38:40.124 --> 00:38:44.957
Is the flower there when the
gardener's planting the seed?

00:38:46.366 --> 00:38:50.159
And that's what...we're going
to examine, the possibility.

00:38:50.159 --> 00:38:51.326
So here we go.

00:38:54.430 --> 00:38:55.821
Okay, good.

00:38:55.821 --> 00:38:59.421
Then he says, what time
am I stopping Tim? 3 5 5.

00:38:59.421 --> 00:39:01.921
(Tim murmurs)

00:39:03.484 --> 00:39:05.514
How much? 10 minutes.

00:39:05.514 --> 00:39:07.708
6 minutes, okay I'm just
trying to keep track.

00:39:07.708 --> 00:39:09.141
,GAL TE RGYU DANG RKYEN RNAMS KYI,

00:39:09.141 --> 00:39:11.307
,TSOGS PA NYID LAS SKYE 'GYUR ZHING,

00:39:11.307 --> 00:39:12.873
,TSOGS LA 'BRAS BU YOD NA NI,

00:39:12.873 --> 00:39:14.700
,JI LTAR TSOGS PA NYID LAS SKYE,

00:39:14.700 --> 00:39:17.918
If the opening of the cafe is present

00:39:17.918 --> 00:39:22.299
in the final convening of
all the causes and conditions

00:39:22.299 --> 00:39:25.180
then why the hell do we
have to open the cafe?

00:39:25.180 --> 00:39:27.053
(students laugh)

00:39:27.053 --> 00:39:28.553
It's already open.

00:39:29.869 --> 00:39:33.005
It's that cute (laughs), it's very cute.

00:39:33.005 --> 00:39:35.147
Then why do you have to open the cafe?

00:39:35.147 --> 00:39:36.747
It's already open.

00:39:36.747 --> 00:39:39.534
If the opening of the cafe is present

00:39:39.534 --> 00:39:43.407
in the convening of
the final requirements,

00:39:43.407 --> 00:39:46.080
then we don't have to
open the cafe, all right?

00:39:46.080 --> 00:39:48.196
,GAL TE RGYU DANG RKYEN RNAMS KYI,

00:39:48.196 --> 00:39:49.546
,TSOGS PA NYID LAS SKYE 'GYUR ZHING,

00:39:49.546 --> 00:39:50.379
,TSOGS LA 'BRAS BU MED NA NI,

00:39:50.379 --> 00:39:52.655
,JI LTAR TSOGS PA NYID LAS SKYE,

00:39:52.655 --> 00:39:55.634
But if the opening of the
cafe is not somehow present

00:39:55.634 --> 00:39:58.706
within this particular
causes and conditions,

00:39:58.706 --> 00:40:01.458
then how could a cafe open?

00:40:01.458 --> 00:40:04.850
Then you would just open
a bookstore or something.

00:40:04.850 --> 00:40:09.552
See, why should a cafe
open when all the causes

00:40:09.552 --> 00:40:12.914
and conditions to open a cafe are present?

00:40:12.914 --> 00:40:16.494
If the opening of the cafe
is somehow not present

00:40:16.494 --> 00:40:19.634
in that final convening of
the causes and conditions,

00:40:19.634 --> 00:40:23.264
because if the opening of
the cafe was not present,

00:40:23.264 --> 00:40:26.685
in some degree, during the final convening

00:40:26.685 --> 00:40:30.047
of the causes and conditions
for the opening of the cafe,

00:40:30.047 --> 00:40:33.970
then when the causes and
conditions finally did their thing,

00:40:33.970 --> 00:40:37.182
it should pop out a
rabbit's turd or something.

00:40:37.182 --> 00:40:38.015
See what I mean?

00:40:38.015 --> 00:40:40.515
Why wouldn't anything pop out?

00:40:41.950 --> 00:40:43.680
I'll say it again.

00:40:43.680 --> 00:40:46.258
If the opening of the
cafe is not in some degree

00:40:46.258 --> 00:40:50.635
present within the final
moment of the convening

00:40:50.635 --> 00:40:53.324
of all the necessities
for the cafe to open,

00:40:53.324 --> 00:40:57.357
then when they do convene,
why should they open a cafe?

00:40:57.357 --> 00:41:00.653
Why shouldn't they open a
rabbit's turd or something?

00:41:00.653 --> 00:41:03.388
I mean, what's...because
there's no relationship.

00:41:03.388 --> 00:41:06.589
There's no necessary relationship.

00:41:06.589 --> 00:41:10.015
There's no more reason
why a cafe should pop out

00:41:10.015 --> 00:41:13.470
than a rabbit's turd, if the
cafe is not in some degree

00:41:13.470 --> 00:41:17.229
present in the final convening
of causes and conditions.

00:41:17.229 --> 00:41:18.221
Okay, got it?

00:41:18.221 --> 00:41:20.143
Or at least, I think
in English we would say

00:41:20.143 --> 00:41:22.045
the potential is somehow there.

00:41:22.045 --> 00:41:24.094
There's more potential
for a cafe to pop out

00:41:24.094 --> 00:41:26.637
then rabbit turd to pop out.

00:41:26.637 --> 00:41:28.813
Something like that.

00:41:28.813 --> 00:41:31.151
But it's...here we go, okay?

00:41:31.151 --> 00:41:33.151
Here comes the question.

00:41:36.668 --> 00:41:38.918
Actually I just covered it.

00:41:46.261 --> 00:41:49.118
TSOGS LA 'BRAS BU RANG BZHIN GYIS YOD NA

00:41:49.118 --> 00:41:53.022
Yeah, if the result of the cafe popping out

00:41:53.022 --> 00:41:56.047
were somehow present the moment before,

00:41:56.047 --> 00:41:59.346
during the time of the final convening

00:41:59.346 --> 00:42:01.968
of all the things you
need to open the cafe,

00:42:01.968 --> 00:42:04.493
then there wouldn't be
a need to open the cafe,

00:42:04.493 --> 00:42:06.578
because it would already be open.

00:42:06.578 --> 00:42:07.901
And it would be redundant.

00:42:07.901 --> 00:42:09.522
He keeps using the word redundant.

00:42:09.522 --> 00:42:12.274
There wouldn't be any
point to opening the cafe

00:42:12.274 --> 00:42:15.191
a second time, a millisecond later.

00:42:18.367 --> 00:42:21.647
Here's a picture. I had
trouble with the picture.

00:42:21.647 --> 00:42:26.647
I couldn't find a picture that
you didn't have to explain.

00:42:26.888 --> 00:42:31.221
But somehow, if the watermelon
were already present

00:42:32.624 --> 00:42:36.124
in the seeds and being together and then,

00:42:37.471 --> 00:42:41.264
why would you have to plant the seeds.

00:42:41.264 --> 00:42:43.679
There wouldn't be any point to it.

00:42:43.679 --> 00:42:46.000
I'm calling this watermelon,

00:42:46.000 --> 00:42:50.750
the watermelon which came from
the seeds that you see now.

00:42:52.929 --> 00:42:55.519
If the watermelon were already present

00:42:55.519 --> 00:42:57.729
in the time of its seeds,

00:42:57.729 --> 00:43:00.959
then why would the seeds
have to do anything?

00:43:00.959 --> 00:43:02.042
Got it?

00:43:03.122 --> 00:43:04.539
All right, hmm...

00:43:11.059 --> 00:43:14.309
And suppose the result is not somehow

00:43:15.474 --> 00:43:19.186
present even as a potential in the final

00:43:19.186 --> 00:43:23.144
convening of the causes and conditions,

00:43:23.144 --> 00:43:25.644
then it could never grow.

00:43:26.499 --> 00:43:29.780
And I don't know, as
desperate I got an apple here.

00:43:29.780 --> 00:43:31.939
You can see the apple.

00:43:31.939 --> 00:43:36.305
It's metal apple. So it
didn't grow from anything.

00:43:36.305 --> 00:43:38.454
Got it? Makes sense?

00:43:38.454 --> 00:43:42.293
If the result is not
present in the convening

00:43:42.293 --> 00:43:44.388
of the causes and conditions,

00:43:44.388 --> 00:43:48.502
then why would it ever create that cause?

00:43:48.502 --> 00:43:50.964
If it's not somehow connected to the final

00:43:50.964 --> 00:43:53.972
coming together of the
causes and conditions,

00:43:53.972 --> 00:43:56.837
which means it pre-exists,

00:43:56.837 --> 00:44:01.087
then why would an apple come out?

00:44:02.035 --> 00:44:05.812
Why wouldn't something else come out?

00:44:05.812 --> 00:44:08.197
And there's another deep problem there.

00:44:08.197 --> 00:44:11.564
Nagarjuna keeps coming
back to the idea that

00:44:11.564 --> 00:44:14.397
if things are not coming from you,

00:44:16.097 --> 00:44:17.460
if things are coming from their own side,

00:44:17.460 --> 00:44:20.839
they cannot change. They
have no reason to change.

00:44:20.839 --> 00:44:23.557
(coughs) I'll say it again.

00:44:23.557 --> 00:44:26.131
Nagarjuna keeps coming back to this theme,

00:44:26.131 --> 00:44:28.881
which you guys can chew on, that

00:44:29.863 --> 00:44:34.530
if the cause is somehow
not present in the causes,

00:44:35.756 --> 00:44:38.506
if the result were self-existent,

00:44:39.382 --> 00:44:41.847
then it couldn't change.

00:44:41.847 --> 00:44:44.745
If something is self-existent,

00:44:44.745 --> 00:44:47.369
it will always have to be the same.

00:44:47.369 --> 00:44:52.369
Because it doesn't depend on
its existence on other things.

00:44:52.975 --> 00:44:57.508
If the jar were self-existent,
then it would never break,

00:44:57.508 --> 00:45:00.728
or it could never be made because
it just existed by itself.

00:45:00.728 --> 00:45:03.241
It didn't exist because of a factory

00:45:03.241 --> 00:45:05.622
or didn't break because I dropped it.

00:45:05.622 --> 00:45:08.088
It would just be all the time the same.

00:45:08.088 --> 00:45:11.766
If it does change, it's not self-existent.

00:45:11.766 --> 00:45:13.094
That's all.

00:45:13.094 --> 00:45:15.785
Let me see if that's a good place to stop.

00:45:15.785 --> 00:45:18.025
That's a great place to stop.

00:45:18.025 --> 00:45:19.358
Okay, Uncle Tim?

00:45:20.214 --> 00:45:23.131
And, we'll do one more class. 

00:45:24.903 --> 00:45:26.653
Thank you guys.

